  "full_response": "DNS resolution is a critical process that connects human-friendly domain names, like www.example.com, to IP addresses that computers use to communicate on the internet. When you type a URL in your browser, several steps unfold behind scenes to translate that domain into an IP address.\n\n1. When you type a URL, your browser first checks its cache to see if it recently resolved that domain to an IP address.\n2. If the IP isn't in the cache, your browser asks the operating system (the computer) to resolve the domain.\n3. The operating then hands this instruction to the network stack of the computer, causing a series of system calls that reach the Data Link Layer of the OS to handle the actual network request.\n4. This request ultimately goes to a DNS resolver on the local internet service provider (ISP). In some locations, DNS requests first go to local resolvers for potentially faster results.\n5. The resolver looks up the domain's IP address in its cache. If it finds the answer, it sends the corresponding IP address back to the operating system.\n6. If the lookup isn't found, the resolver receives a reciprocity-routable (RR) query for the root DNS servers. The root servers direct the resolver to a Top-Level Domain (TLD) server that has the authority for the \".\", \".org\", or \".net\" portion of the URL.\n7. The resolver then questions this TLD server, which responds with the IP address of a DNS server directly in charge of the domain in question or provides a further referral.\n8. This process repeats until the resolver finds the specific Domain Name Server (DNS server) that answered the root TLD query. The resolver can glean information from any previously satisfied zone or where further referrals have been required.\n9. Finally, the sends back the requested IP address in a DNS Message, which then reaches your operating system, where the IP associate to that URL is cached saved for future access. It then contacts the IP address and follows the path to establish a new connection and establishes your browser.\n10. Once connected, the browser fetches the content from the requested domain and finally displays it on your screen. If any step in this process fails, the signal will stop the entire process. In case of any failures, the browser will prompt the user to check the URL or manually retrieve the content.\n\n\nIn summary, DNS resolution is a system where the network hierarchy resolves the given domain to an IP address in a top-down approach. This involves several layers of communication between",
